\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Poland Auctions 2-Year Bonds  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 07:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 0900/0800  Hungary January Industrial Producer Prices \par            Seen up 1.2% on year\par  \par 1200/1100  Poland Auctions PLN2.5B Of 2-Yr Bonds \par  \par 1630/1530  Hungary Issues Next Wk Security Issue Sked \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 3.8945-51  3.9125-85  -0.46  3.9126  3.8921   -4.54 \par EUR/HUF 241.60-75  241.60-75   0.00  Untraded today   -1.44 \par EUR/CZK 29.62-67   29.620-50   0.00  29.630  29.650   -2.57 \par EUR/SKK 37.843-92  37.843-92   0.00  Untraded today   -2.25 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THE POLISH ZLOTY appears likely to continue its steady advance Wednesday, with the next resistance point against the euro at PLN3.85, says a Bank Pekao analyst.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The zloty was stronger against the euro Tuesday, after recovering from an early dip. The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at PLN3.8785, compared with PLN3.8838 midday and PLN3.8970 late Monday. Movement was slow as traders were reluctant to take short positions after resistance at PLN3.90 was breached, but real money flows, including E.U. funds, were exerting steady pressure, a Bank Pekao analyst said. Against the dollar, the zloty was weaker, at PLN2.9410, compared with PLN2.9405 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT should linger around HUF241.50 Wednesday, says CIB, although the bank adds that there is a chance the forint will break below that level. CIB says "some investors are trying to push the EUR/HUF out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band in order to shake off at least some of the burdens of their option books." It expects this to happen in around two weeks' time, when the National Bank of Hungary will be absent from the market due to a public holiday.  \par \par \uc2 The forint strengthened Tuesday in low-activity trading. The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading around HUF241.85 against the euro mid-afternoon Tuesday, compared with HUF242.15 at Tuesday's open. The forint was expected to continue to firm until option deals are triggered nearer to the edge of the floating band at HUF240.01 against the euro, a dealer said. This was expected to happen outside \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opening hours.  \par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA should continue to appreciate Wednesday, but at a more leisurely pace, traders say. The koruna is likely to ignore continuing political uncertainty in the Czech Republic, most traders said. Raiffeisen Bank suggests this trend will continue as long as the Cesky Telecom privatization doesn't appear to be under threat. Any sign of early elections is likely to provoke a deep correction, the bank adds. If the koruna breaches CZK29.500 against the euro this week or next, this is likely to result in profit-taking, traders add.  \par \par \uc2 The koruna was stronger Tuesday, mimicking gains made by both the Polish zloty and Slovak koruna. The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at CZK29.647-CZK29.677 against the euro, compared with CZK29.680 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS are likely to take cues Wednesday from the government's PLN2.5 billion auction of two-year bonds. An ABN AMRO trader says yields are still trying to find their equilibrium level after falling 70 basis points in two weeks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local bonds were weaker Tuesday as profit-taking and flagging bunds took their toll. The five-year average yield was up at 5.43%, compared with 5.34% late Monday. The two-year bond was yielding 5.37%, up from 5.30% Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS are likely to see some repositioning Wednesday, as investors make way for Thursday's three-year and 15-year bond auction, which is expected to see strong demand, traders say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian bonds were lower Tuesday, with yields up five basis points across the curve after a big drop in yields Monday, a dealer said. The three-year benchmark was yielding 7.99%-7.96%, while the five-year paper was yielding between 7.03%-7.86%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS should trade quietly Wednesday, taking cues from core markets, as investors look to Friday's key U.S. payroll data, and then to next week's fourth-quarter domestic gross domestic product data and February inflation figures for a steer on the likelihood of new rate cuts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local bonds were steady Tuesday, with little reaction to the publication of the Finance Ministry's quarterly bond-issue calendar. Some traders were, however, surprised that the government plans to offer CZK30 billion of local CZK-denominated paper as it is also issuing euro-denominated bonds next week. Yields were almost unchanged.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                     2088.80   loss   3.53   dn 0.2% \par BUX                      18673.60   gain 290.64   up 1.6% \par PX-D                      2936.00   gain  42.5    up 1.5% \par  \par DJ STOXX EU Enlarged 15   2233.19   gain  13.76   up 0.6% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW, stocks are likely to keep a close eye on the political situation Wednesday, after a spokesman for Prime Minister Marek Belka said late Tuesday that the prime minister will make an "important political statement" Thursday. Speculation has been rife that Belka might bolt from the ruling Left Democratic Alliance, or SLD, to join a new centrist party formed Sunday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local stocks were weaker Tuesday. The WIG20 fell 0.2%, although many blue chips were unchanged. Blue-chip mining stock KGHM fell 1.8%, while Bank Pekao was 0.7% lower, and Orbis was down 1.3%, after reporting a narrowing of its consolidated net loss in the fourth quarter. BRE Bank was the lone blue-chip gainer, rising 2%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST, local equities should continue to see an inflow of money from investment funds Wednesday that will push prices higher, an HVB trader says.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian stocks continued their climb Tuesday, with the BUX index ending 1.6% higher. OTP added 3.5%, much of it in the last hour of trading, after Moody's Investors Service Inc. indicated they may upgrade the company's rating. Matav rose 0.3% after a late rally Monday, with 1.6 million shares changing hands. MOL was up 0.9%, and Richter was flat.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE, Czech stocks are poised to continue their rally Wednesday, with no corporate events scheduled to give the market pause, says a trader at Patria Finance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local stocks rose Tuesday, with most blue chips higher. The PX-D was up 1.5%. Erste Bank was the session's clear winner, soaring 3.7% after Deutsche Bank raised its recommendation on the stock to buy from hold and boosted its price target. Unipetrol was close behind, up 3.6% on continued demand from local retail investors. Zentiva ended 1.3% higher. Only Cesky Telecom bucked the trend, falling 0.3% after trading in a narrow range between CZK395 and CZK396 a share for most of the day. [ 03-02-05 0128ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OVERNIGHT NEWS \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prime Minister Marek Belka said in a magazine interview to be published Wednesday that Poland could hold early elections on June 19, and that he wouldn't rule out leaving his governing Democratic Left party to join a new centrist party.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be forced to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market over the coming days as the forint trengthens, a survey of 17 analysts by online economic news agency Portfolio.hu showed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Romania's oil and gas company Petrom SA (SNP.RO) in 2005 will maintain production at the same level as last year, and will invest some EUR200 million to avoid an output decline in the coming years, the company said in a statement.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of the European Union Parliament and the bloc's enlargement commissioner urged Romania Tuesday to eradicate corruption from its public administration. "The European Parliament has found that what we defined as corruption last December - the inefficient functioning of the public administration - continues to be a problem" in Romania, said Josep Borrell, who heads the E.U. Parliament.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrea Chipman, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 207 842 9319; andrea.chipman@dowjones.com [ 03-02-05 0129ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050302e1320000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
