\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Poland Nov Indus Output, PPI Eyed  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2004\par 07:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 1600/1500  Poland Nov Industrial Output \par            Seen up 3.3% on year \par  \par 1600/1500  Poland Nov PPI Data \par            Seen up 7.7% on year \par  \par N/A        Hungary Nov Budget Deficit In Detail \par            Prelim deficit HUF44.4B, seen HUF107B in Dec \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 4.1631-71  4.1670-10  -0.09  4.1668  4.1558  -11.53 \par EUR/HUF 245.65-05  246.20-55  -0.22  246.18  245.50   -5.96 \par EUR/CZK 30.562-72  30.597-47  -0.11  30.632  30.572   -5.09 \par EUR/SKK 38.760-10  38.760-10   0.00  38.760  38.745   -5.91 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THE POLISH ZLOTY retains positive sentiment despite its slight decline Thursday, traders said. The zloty could take direction Friday from November industrial output and PPI data.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The zloty was a touch weaker Thursday. The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at PLN3.11 against the dollar, compared to PLN3.10 Wednesday and at PLN4.1665 against the euro, compared with PLN4.1550 Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT should remain around HUF245 to HUF246 until Monday's key interest rate meeting, with no significant weakness seen, an IEB dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The forint firmed Thursday from levels earlier in the day, as markets calmed following comment from Finance Minister Tibor Draskovics which indicated the views of the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are moving closer on key rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues. The forint was trading at HUF245.70-90 against the euro, compared with levels above HUF246 earlier Thursday, and HUF245.70 Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA could continue to decline Friday as investors seek to close positions ahead of year-end holidays, a trader said.  \par \par \uc2 The koruna was weaker Thursday on profit taking, after gains earlier this month. The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at CZK30.627-.677 against the euro Thursday, compared with CZK30.61 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS will take cues Friday from November industrial output and PPI data, in what market participants are viewing as the last activity before the holiday lull sets in.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local bonds were steady Thursday, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strong hints no interest rate increases are pending were offset by profit taking on bunds. Yields on the two-year and five-year bonds were unchanged from late Wednesday at 6.42% and 6.32%, respectively.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS are expected to be subdued Friday, with little movement expected before Monday's rate decision. Most investors are already positioned for a 50 basis point interest rate cut, although the possibility of a 100 basis-point cut can't be excluded, said a Deutsche Bank trader.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local bonds gained slightly Thursday. Yields were down a few basis points across the curve. Demand was concentrated in the middle segment, with the 2005 and 2008 paper the biggest gainers. At Thursday's 12-month treasury bill sale, the average yield fell to 8.52% from 8.87% at the previous auction.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS should experience slow trading, with little significant movement expected Friday and next week ahead of the Christmas holidays.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local bonds stabilized Thursday, after Wednesday's losses. Yields remained within a two basis point range in either direction from Wednesday's closing levels, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                     1922.19  gain  33.11   up 1.8% \par BUX                      14707.83  gain 182.59   up 1.3% \par PX-D                      2538.9   gain   0.4    unch \par  \par DJ STOXX EU Enlarged 15   1802.76  gain  22.52   up 1.3% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW, local equities should follow up with more gains Friday, although volumes should be lighter, said a trader at BRE Bank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local stock prices made healthy gains Thursday on heavy volumes and strong interest from foreign investors, according to BRE Bank. The WIG20 index was up 1.8% on the day, and blue chips made gains across the board. Recently listed TVN led gainers, rising 7.3% on the day. Media peer Agora ended 4% higher, while BPH Bank and TPSA climbed 3.9%, and 1.6%, respectively.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST, local stocks could take cues from the forint Friday, as the BUX continues heading for record-breaking highs, with no obvious catalyst for profit taking.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian stocks rose Thursday, with nearly all blue chips ending higher. The BUX rose 1.3%. Banking stock OTP led gainers, ending 2.1% higher, while oil and gas company MOL gained 1.5% and pharmaceutical Egis climbed 0.9%. Matav ended flat.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE stocks should stay well-supported Friday, with no correction expected ahead of Christmas, according to a strategist at Komercni Banka.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local stocks ended flat Thursday, after rising earlier in the session and correcting back to opening levels later in the afternoon. Zentiva was the strongest mover of the session, ending 1% higher. Philip Morris Czech Republic rose 0.8% on the day, while Komercni Banka climbed 0.3%. CEZ, and Cesky Telecom ended slightly lower, after hitting positive territory earlier in the day. CEZ was down 0.3%, while Cesky Telecom fell 0.4%. [ 17-12-04 0633GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OVERNIGHT NEWS \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Portuguese-led consortium has been granted a 35-year concession to extend and operate a major Bulgarian highway linking the Serbian border with the Black Sea port of Burgas. The concession was given to the Automagistrala Tracia AD consortium, which is 51-percent owned by the Portuguese companies companies MSF-Moniz da Maia, Serra&Fortunato-Empreiteiros SA, Lena Engenharia e Construcoes S.A. and Somague Concessoes e Servicos SA (SMGAE.LB). The remaining 49% is owned by Bulgarian state companies.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary enjoys the sole right to the Tokaj wine trademark, a European court judge ruled Wednesday. Italy had requested similar treatment for its wines cultivated from the Tocai grape in the Friuli-Venezia Giulia region.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Preliminary inquiries have begun into the shipping of excess \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  grain from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to Belgium when the country runs out of storage space, traders and industry sources said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish low-cost carrier Air Polonia filed a complaint in Warsaw against an Irish investor whose unexpected pullout from a deal led the company to suspend all flights and start bankruptcy proceedings.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrea Chipman, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 207 842 9319; andrea.chipman@dowjones.com [ 17-12-04 0633GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091  \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020041217e0ch00003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
