\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Hungary's Draskovics lays credibilty on the line.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By David Chance   \par \uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2004\par 16:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, Sept 9 (Reuters) - Hungary's Finance Minister Tibor Draskovics has put his credibility on the line over the 2004 budget, but analysts question whether the man who promised so much will deliver any more than his predecessors.   \par \par \uc2 Draskovics has said repeatedly that government revenues will pick up towards the end of the year, allowing Hungary to patch over holes in the budget and deliver a deficit no bigger than 4.6 percent of gross domestic product (GDP).   \par \par \uc2 But revenues have continued to be weaker than forecast, and were 23 billion forints less than planned in August. This has prompted a rethink of methodology used to forecast value added tax receipts, and unnerved investors who are banking on Hungary cutting the deficit so it can join the euro in 2010.   \par \par \uc2 "The outcome of this year's deficit is important not only because markets regard it as a test of credibility, but also because this is the basis for next year's calculations," said Tamas Vojnits, economic analyst at Hungary's biggest bank, OTP.   \par \par \uc2 The problem is that it is deja vu, all over again.   \par \par \uc2 Draskovics could become the victim of the political pressure which claimed the scalp of predecessor Csaba Laszlo, if there is a hat trick of missed budgets with a deficit overshoot in 2004.   \par \par \uc2 No one in financial markets really expects Draskovics to achieve his promised deficit of 4.6 percent and analysts say investors will tolerate any number under five percent.   \par \par \uc2 Only an overshoot of five percent or more will provoke a market selloff, they say, or if Draskovics makes creeping upward revisions to the deficit like those made by Laszlo in 2003.   \par \par \uc2 Laszlo, an able technocrat, presided over a rise in the budget deficit to 6.0 percent of GDP in 2003 from an original target of 4.5 percent.   \par \par \uc2 He could not withstand pressure to spend more from the ruling Socialist Party and a mismatch between \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and the fiscal side triggered three forint crises in 2003.   \par \par \uc2 BACK TO 1996, NEW FACE IN 2004   \par \par \uc2 The Socialists have a bad record on budget discipline. In 1996 then-prime minister Gyula Horn baulked at the radical reforms of finance minister Lajos Bokros and kicked him out of office - yet he still managed to lose a 1998 election.   \par \par \uc2 In 2004, there is a political unknown in the form of Ferenc Gyurcsany, a popular politician who is set to become prime minister later this month. He wants to make Hungary a fairer society and to rebuild support for the Socialist Party.   \par \par \uc2 Gyurcsany has so far talked the kind of language markets like, stressing continuity, but no specifics.   \par \par \uc2 If Hungary wants to come on target with a 4.6 percent deficit, spending cuts in the region of 160 billion forints or around 0.8 percentage points would be needed, based on an overshoot in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forecast of a deficit of 5.4 percent of GDP.   \par \par \uc2 There is some wriggle-room.   \par \par \uc2 "Spending items pencilled in for November-December could also be delayed until January 2005, a 'trick' which has been used before," CSFB analyst Olivier Desbarres said.   \par \par \uc2 Neither Gyurcsany nor Draskovics will talk numbers, although analysts said Draskovics gave his clearest hint there would be an overshoot when he told Reuters on Wednesday that the finance ministry would look at VAT revenue projections.   \par \par \uc2 But other politicians have at least started to talk about a potential overshoot, among them Gabor Kuncze, head of the smaller party in the ruling Socialist-led coalition, the Free Democrats, which has four ministerial posts.   \par \par \uc2 "If there is around a 0.3/0.4 percentage point difference, it seems that this has already been priced in by markets," Kuncze told public service TV on Thursday. "However if the difference turns out to be significantly bigger, which I do not believe it will, then there is need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2 That leaves investors wondering if the 11 percent \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  pays in official interest rates is enough to reflect the risk.   \par \par \uc2 For the moment it is. "The key to the forint's stability remains the high carry versus the euro zone, which attracts large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows that in turn finance the 9 percent of GDP current account deficit," said CSFB's Desbarres.   \par \par \uc2 The forint is also underpinned by Hungarian companies and individuals borrowing cheaply in euros and then converting those euros into forints, and barring a massive external shock, there is no sign that will change.   \par \par \uc2 "The last time the Hungarian private sector significantly unwound its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  shorts/forint long positions was in 1998, following the Russian debt default," Desbarres noted. FOR FACTBOX ON BUDGET PLEASE DOUBLE CLICK.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040909e099007kx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
