\par\par \b \uc2 Hungary's Jarai: Exchange Rate Regime To Remain In Place  \b0\par\par\uc2 842 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2003\par 10:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST (Dow Jones)--The National Bank of Hungary will maintain a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  preference range of 250 to 260 forints ($1=HUF22.97) against the euro, but it will be more flexible about this range than previously, Governor Zsigmond Jarai said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "We'll treat the HUF250 to HUF260 range with more flexibility since we've already agreed on the 2005 inflation goal and the inflation target is our chief goal," Jarai told a press conference.  \par \par \uc2 "It's possible that in three or six months a different exchange rate will be needed for this inflation goal," Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 However, in the short term, the bank will allow little deviation from this preference range and will adjust rate policy to keep the forint at this level.  \par \par \uc2 At around 1000 GMT, the forint was trading at between 264 and 265 against the euro after the bank raised its key deposit rate to 12.5% from 9.5% to prevent further weakening.  \par \par \uc2 But for now, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only tolerate small and temporary deviations from the preference range, but the current \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening isn't small and temporary, but reflects a radical change in investor sentiment, Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 "The past few days prove that there's a systematic change in investor sentiment toward Hungary. Not only short-term hedge funds are behind the current weakening, but portfolio investors as well," Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 The forint weakened to around HUF269 against the euro early Friday after tumbling earlier this week from HUF261.50 last week as portfolio investors have lost confidence in Hungary.  \par \par \uc2 "They have become insecure about Hungary's economic fundamentals... and we can only reinstate their trust by this high interest rate level," Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 Hungary's current account deficit this year will be higher than the bank expected at the start of this year, and the budget deficit, while on a narrowing path, will also be high. Furthermore, Hungary finances its current account deficit mostly via foreign portfolio investments so portfolio investors' confidence in Hungary is vital.  \par \par \uc2 "Because of the imbalances, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation expectations increased among portfolio investors. That's why we need to hike rates this much," Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 The current interest rate provides a 6% forward-looking return adjusted for 2004 inflation, a level "considerably high but not unprecedented" for Hungary, Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 The current exchange rate of the forint will remain in place until the country joins ERMII, the exchange rate regime that serves as the ante-room to euro-zone membership.  \par \par \uc2 Hungary's target date to adopt the euro of Jan. 1, 2008, was a consideration for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it decided about Friday's rate hike, Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considers the target date for euro membership is a realistic goal and Hungary could still achieve it. Despite its current economic woes, an improvement in its fundamentals is on the cards from next year, Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 "We can still enter the euro zone Jan. 1 2008," Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 Hungary will join the European Union in May 2004 and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to enter ERMII as soon as possible after that, he added.  \par \par \uc2 "A tight economic policy is needed for that and we'll pursue a tight monetary policy to reach our euro goals," Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 High inflation and a wide budget deficit are Hungary's main hurdles on its road to adopting the euro. Reducing inflation is a chief goal of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 When deciding about the Friday's hefty rate hike, "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considered whether a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or higher interest rates lead to greater economic tensions, and we decided that a weaker forint would, as it would pose a threat to the inflation goals," Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate is the chief tool in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  for reducing inflation," Jarai added.  \par \par \uc2 Jarai rejected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the open markets as a possible way of stemming the forint's weakness, saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only control temporary swings in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Minister of Finance Laszlo Csaba was present at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate meeting as usual, Jarai said, although the bank didn't seek government approval for the rate hike because it is independent he added.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects the economy to start improving next year on a recovery in the European Union and especially Germany, Hungary's most significant export market. Even if economic growth doesn't accelerate from this year's level of about 3.5%, it is expected to become more balanced. Exports are expected to drive growth, rather than domestic demand and household consumption which have led the economy in the past two years.  \par \par \uc2 If the forint and bond markets recover, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will reduce interest rates, Jarai said. The amount of Hungarian government bonds denominated in forints in foreign hands is worth about EUR8 billion, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not impossible to cut rates if investor confidence returns," Jarai said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Margit Feher, Dow Jones Newswires; +361-267-0622; margit.feher@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031128dzbs000dn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
