\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Hungary c.bank, finmin try to talk up weak forint.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Krisztina Than   \par \uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2003\par 16:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, Nov 25 (Reuters) - Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance minister attempted to talk up the sliding forint on Tuesday, saying the weakness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds was temporary and not justified by the economy's fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2 By 1501 GMT the forint firmed to 260.95/261.45 to the euro, from below 262 earlier on Tuesday, but remained highly volatile, still trading outside the bank's preferred range of 250-260.   \par \par \uc2 The forint started falling on Monday on a sell-off in government bonds and extended losses on Tuesday, as bond sales continued, with yields surging over 100 basis points in two days and expectations for an interest rate hike lingering.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) said the forint should trade at 250-260 to the euro for its four percent end-2005 inflation target to be achievable, adding that it believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's present falls were "temporary."   \par \par \uc2 The NBH said it would not react to temporary and small fluctuations of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but "would take the necessary steps if it believes market developments jeopardise the achievability of the inflation target".   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the sell-off in Hungarian bonds was mainly due to a spillover of jitters from Poland where the zloty and bonds eased after last week's poorly-bid auction.   \par \par \uc2 But they added the risk appetite of big foreign investors or end users - key holders of Hungary's bonds and thus financiers of the country's wide current account deficit - was very low and markets would remain highly vulnerable to contagion in the region, mainly from Poland, and shifts in sentiment.   \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Csaba Laszlo also said the markets' weakness was temporary and not due to fundamental reasons.   \par \par \uc2 He told Reuters the budget vote in Parliament was progressing as planned and the government's plan to cut the public sector deficit to 3.8 percent of GDP next year, from five percent this year, would be approved later on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 YIELDS SURGE, MARKETS ON EDGE   \par \par \uc2 The yield on Hungary's 2008/C bond was up 60 basis points at 9.80/10.00 percent, while the 2006/G was quoted at 10.30/50 percent, up 55 basis points.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said sentiment in the next few days will be key to auctions of five-year 2009/C and ten-year 2014/C bonds on Thursday, which will be closely watched by markets.   \par \par \uc2 Poor demand at the auctions may trigger a further sell-off and forint weakness and may eventually force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise key interest rates when its Monetary Council holds its next regular meeting next Monday.   \par \par \uc2 "I believe the forint will remain very volatile in the next few days...but the bank must avoid hiking rates as that would only fuel expectations for further rate hikes," one trader said.   \par \par \uc2 This week's is the second mini-crisis in Hungary's financial markets this month. Early in November a sell-off in bonds weakened the forint and forced the NBH to steady the markets by buying bonds in an unorthodox form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have been burnt in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's volatile financial markets three times this year, as the forint has gyrated between levels of 234 and 270 to the euro.   \par \par \uc2 Hungary will auction 30 billion forints ($134.7 million) worth of five-year and 20 billion worth of 10-year bonds on Thursday, down from the usual offer of 40 and 30 billion. The the government has said it will reduce supply until January to boost demand and improve liquidity.   \par \par \uc2 In January Hungary will issue one billion euros worth of eurobonds.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031125dzbp0030p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
