\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Hungary Central Bank \highlight\uc2 : To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In Bond Market Soon  \b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2003\par 09:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST (Dow Jones)--The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the government bond market, to stem the sharp rise in yields and provide liquidity, a spokesman said Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "From now on, we'll enter the market as buyers of bonds. The market is illiquid and the yields are high," Gabor Missura told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 Missura declined to specify which maturity the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is targeting.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond traders said that, as of 0845 GMT, they hadn't seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in either the bond or the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Bond yields were 20-30 basis points higher than late Friday, after a rise of more than 100 basis points last week on strong selling by nonresidents.  \par \par \uc2 The forint is trading at HUF263.50-.80 ($1=HUF261.95) against the euro, significantly weaker than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's preferred range of HUF250-260.  \par \par \uc2 "The forint was pushed from HUF261.50 to HUF264.40 without a single deal struck in it. Bids are higher and higher but there are no offers," a dealer at a Hungarian commercial bank said.  \par \par \uc2 Bond traders also said deals are thin on the ground.  \par \par \uc2 "The (yields on) three- and five-year maturities are 20-30 basis points higher, but hardly any deals have been struck," a trader at Raiffeisen Bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the country's economic fundamentals don't justify the past few days' rise in the long end of the yield curve. The government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain committed to plans to adopt the euro Jan. 1, 2008, the bank said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hopes that the current market disorder will correct shortly," the bank said, repeating its statement Friday that it considers the developments temporary.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the reason for the past two days' weakness is that some market participants needed to reshuffle their investment portfolios after falls in Polish and the Russian markets. Their selling led to stop-loss sell orders, which deepened the Hungarian market's losses, the bank added.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement comes just hours before its Monetary Council is due to publish its interest rate decision, at 1300 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Market expectations of a rate hike have strengthened on the recent weakness, whereas until Thursday, no-one had expected the bank to raise rates. While most economists and traders still say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably leave its key rate at 9.50%, forecasts of a 50-100-basis-point rate hike are increasing.  \par \par \uc2 -By Margit Feher, Dow Jones Newswires; +361-267-0622; margit.feher@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031103dzb3000gn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
