\par\par \b \uc2 ECB's Tumpel Gugerell:Euro Gains Ground In Ctrl, E Europe  \b0\par\par\uc2 167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2003\par 12:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--The euro is becoming increasingly important in Central and Eastern European countries that are applying to join the European Union, Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell, a member of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governing council, told a banking conference Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Candidate countries have issued euro-denominated bonds totaling more than EUR30 billion, and one country, Montenegro, has actually pegged its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the euro, she said.  \par \par \uc2 The share of the euro in accession countries' official reserves is around 50%, higher than in other regions.  \par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Romania the euro is the official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tumpel-Gugerell said.  \par \par \uc2 The use of the euro for invoicing sales of Russian oil in the region is also under discussion, she added.  \par \par \uc2 Montenegro adopted the euro peg unilaterally to boost the credibility of its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said in response to a question.  \par \par \uc2 -By Aviva Freudmann, Dow Jones Newswires; +49 69 29725 500; aviva.freudmann@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031027dzar000ha\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
