\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary cbanker says no immediate forint target. \b0\par\par\uc2 419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2003\par 12:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BASEL, Switzerland, June 30 (Reuters) - Hungary has no immediate exchange rate target for the forint following its recent shift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fluctuation bands but would be satisifed to see it return to the previous target range, a top central banker said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Peter Adamecz, deputy president of the National Bank of Hungary, told reporters that with hindsight the 2.26 percent lowering of the forint's parity band had not been well timed. \par \par \uc2  "With hindsight, we can safely say that it was not particularly good timing for the change in the frame for the exchange rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. It was not meant to be an actual devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said on the sidelines of the annual meeting of the Bank for International Settlements. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the forint could return to the previous target range of 250-260 to the euro, Adamecz said: \par \par \uc2  "It might come back. We have not an immediate exchange rate target. \par \par \uc2  "We are just looking at certain ranges which would be more suitable from the point of view of the inflation target for 2004." \par \par \uc2  Asked if the bank had any other range, he said, "We don't have any other range in mind. We would be satisfied to have the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in that range that you mentioned." \par \par \uc2  Adamecz said the shift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band was not related to any preparations by \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  to join the ERM-2 exchange rate mechanism prior to adopting the euro. \par \par \uc2  Hungary is one of 10 countries, mostly from central and eastern Europe, preparing to join the European Union next May. By treaty, the new members will be obliged eventually to join the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Under ERM-2, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may fluctuate by 15 percent either side of a central parity point. \par \par \uc2  EU Monetary Affairs Commissioner Pedro Solbes recently indicated that countries in ERM2 would have to restrict fluctuations to 2.25 percent but later said this was just an assessment criterion for euro entry. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether exchange rate criteria had been discussed in last week's meeting in Frankfurt of the EU aspirant countries and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Adamecz said," It was in fact a confirmation of what we already knew." \par \par \uc2  "It was already clear that the exchange rate fluctuation band in ERM-2 is similar to what we have already in Hungary but there is a narrower tolerance band for the definition of exchange rate stability." \par \par \uc2  Adamecz said Hungary currently had no timetable for ERM2 entry. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030630dz6u00kuq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
