\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Hungary cbank not to prop up forint, but warns. \b0\par\par\uc2 438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2003\par 18:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates with warning in c.bank statement in paras 3, 6, 7-10, analyst comment in 11-13.) \par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, June 4 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the forint after sharp falls as budget adjustments and improved competitiveness prospects help it meet inflation targets, Governor Zsigmond Jarai said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "There is no need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the current exchange rate is far from the edge of the band and even further from the new edge," Jarai told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  But in a statement released by the Monetary Council later, the bank warned that after a devaluation of parity in the forint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's band it still had the means to defend inflation targets. \par \par \uc2  The government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced earlier on Wednesday that they were devaluing the forint's parity rate to 282.36 against the euro from a previous 276.10 to boost job creation and competitiveness ahead of EU accession next year. \par \par \uc2  Jarai said the forint should fluctuate around 250 to the euro, below earlier levels of around 245, to help achieve inflation targets. \par \par \uc2  When Jarai spoke, the forint traded slightly lower then 256 against the euro, but after the news conference its slide continued. \par \par \uc2  WARNING IN LATER STATEMENT \par \par \uc2  In a statement issued later the bank said the government's request to shift the central parity was acceptable because "under the present economic circumstances, considering the impacts of a disinflationary international environment, the inflation targets of around 4.5-5.0 percent for end-2003 and 2.5-4.5 percent for end-2004 were achievable provided that the forint's exchange rate stabilises at around 250 to the euro." \par \par \uc2  But the bank added that should this not happen, it still had the tools to tighten monetary policy if it sees its inflation targets at risk. \par \par \uc2  "The Monetary Council believes...that in the new exchange rate band it still has enough room of manoeuvre, in case there is a threat to the inflation targets, to help economic processes move in way which promote further successful disinflation," the bank added. \par \par \uc2  At 1555 GMT the forint was quoted at 267.65/268.15. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the bank will have to raise its key interest rates if the forint does not firm back to around 250 from its present low levels of 266, to which it plunged after the devaluation was announced. \par \par \uc2  "Should verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain ineffective, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to hike interest rates," DZ Bank said. \par \par \uc2  Hungary's key two-week deposit rate stands at 6.5 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030604dz6400vc0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
