\par\par \b \uc2  FACTBOX-Hungarian forint's bumpy road towards ERM-2. \b0\par\par\uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2003\par 17:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BUDAPEST, June 4 (Reuters) - Hungary on Wednesday devalued\par central parity in its Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM 2)-type\par forint band by 2.2 percent to 282.36 to the euro from 276.10.\par Hungary is alone among 10 states due to join the European\par Union next May to have already adopted an ERM-type band.\par Hungary plans to adopt the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  euro in 2007. A\par key condition of that is for the forint to stay in a 30-percent\par wide band for at least two years without adjustment.\par Following is a chronology and facts about Hungary's road to\par ERM-2.\par FORINT BAND\par May 4, 2001 - Flotation band changed for first time in over\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par six years. Forint swiftly gains over 10 percentage points to 245\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  to the euro, boosted also by EU convergence inflows. \par \par \uc2  October 1, 2001 - Forint's crawling peg devaluations lifted \par \par \uc2  and parity rate set at 276.1 to the euro in +/-15 percent band. \par \par \uc2  Previous band had been +/-2.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  January, 2003 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) fends off hot money \par \par \uc2  attack against the top of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, buying 5.2 billion \par \par \uc2  euros in the market and slashing interest rates. \par \par \uc2  May 23, 2003 - NBH says it resold 3.8 billion euros and \par \par \uc2  stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . By June 4, forint falls \par \par \uc2  four percent to below 256 to the euro - lower than levels after \par \par \uc2  January's speculative attack. \par \par \uc2  June 4, 2003 - Government and NBH shift central parity to \par \par \uc2  282.36 against the euro - effectively devaluing forint by over \par \par \uc2  2.2 percent Forint falls to 265. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par INFLATION TARGETING\par June 12, 2001 - NBH launches policy of implicit inflation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par targeting to underline inflation-cutting impact of stronger\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par forint. Previously, the bank did not set inflation targets. The\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par new policy assumed Hungary would join euro zone in January,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2006, having trimmed inflation to about two percent for 2004-05.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par July 12, 2002 - New pro-growth centre-left government and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NBH change euro zone entry target date to 2007 as inflation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par targets rise. Official ERM-2 and euro zone target dates due to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par be set in second half 2003.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LIBERALISATION\par Jan 1, 1996 - Forint made convertible for current account\par deals according to International Monetary Fund (IMF) criteria,\par but short-term capital movements remain restricted.\par June 15, 2001 - NBH and then ruling centre-right cabinet\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par lift restrictions on short-term cross-border capital movements,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par saying liberalisation needed to allow domestic firms and foreign\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par investors to hedge \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risks boosted by wider forint band.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  restrictions lifted, opening up new business\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par opportunities for foreign investors, including trade in forint\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par derivatives and Hungarian short-term debt. Flurry of forint\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par denominated Eurobonds shows international investors bet on\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's future EU convergence.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  BAND WIDTH\par 1992, July 12     - 0.6 percent\par 1994, June 1      - 1.0 percent\par 1994, August 5    - 2.5 percent\par 1994, December 22 - 4.5 percent\par 2001, May 4       - 30 percent\par FORINT DEVALUATIONS\par Hungary devalued the forint in 23 one-off moves between\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par January 1, 1990 and March 13, 1995. The nine percentage point\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par devaluation on March 13, 1995 was the second largest move after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a 15 percent devaluation on January 7, 1991.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The pre-announced monthly crawling peg devaluation rate,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par launched in March 1995 at 1.9 percent, was cut 14 times before\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par being lifted on October 1, 2001.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Central parity rate in the 30-percent-wide band launched in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2001 was devalued by 2.2 percent on June 4, 2003, to 282.36\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par against the euro from 276.10.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030604dz6400u3f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
