\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-EU votes,  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  seen reviving C.Europe rate cut cycle. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sandor Peto \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2003\par 15:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, May 6 (Reuters) - Expectations of interest rate cuts in central Europe should continue to strengthen as several countries hold referendums on EU membership in the next few weeks, analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  They said political, domestic and external economic factors and firming \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would all point in that direction. \par \par \uc2  So would any continued drop in eurozone debt yields which would make the assets of countries which expect to join the EU next May more attractive. \par \par \uc2  "In most states, interest rate cut expectations have strengthened," said Zsolt Papp, analyst at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "We expect the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) will cut later this year, and inflation rates are also lower than expected." \par \par \uc2  Central Europe's main \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have rebounded in recent days, driven in part by a stronger euro and falling eurozone yields, while government bond auctions have seen strong demand. \par \par \uc2  Slovakia's stronger crown is seen prompting interest rate cuts after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, a Polish zloty rally could also trigger cuts, and that could eventually pull Hungary into the cycle as well, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, is seen stopping short of trimming interest rates as there are concerns that a deflation period could end soon and low interest rates would not be sufficient safeguard against inflationary pressure. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  RISES BACK \par \par \uc2  Lower world oil prices have helped EU candidates keep inflation, a major concern ahead of EU accession, in check, while recent falls in EU debt yields made their high-yielding fixed income assets increasingly attractive, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We see scope for all the regions' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen, all have had reasons for being held back recently," said Koon Chow, emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at CSFB in London. \par \par \uc2  "We've had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Slovakia, verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry, political uncertainty in Poland and a deteriorating trade and current account deficit in the Czech Republic." \par \par \uc2  Referendums on EU membership could add to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength in the region, risking a revival of surges late last year which governments feared would slow economic convergence with the wealthier EU. \par \par \uc2  Of 10 candidates, Malta, Slovenia and Hungary have already voted to join the expanding 15-nation EU. Lithuania votes this weekend, followed by Slovakia, Poland, the Czech Republic, Estonia and Latvia. Cyprus is not expected to hold a vote. \par \par \uc2  The June 7-8 referendum in Poland, by far the biggest EU candidate, may attract strong inflows if the result goes in favour of membership as the polls indicate is likely. \par \par \uc2  "Poland's case is a little more uncertain and, if there is a 'yes' vote, strong capital inflows may start, not only in Poland, but across the whole region," said ABN's Papp. \par \par \uc2  SLOVAKIA MAY CUT FIRST \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Slovakia may be the first to cut interest rates after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought 200 million euros in the market on Monday to curb the crown . \par \par \uc2  The bank may bring forward its end-month meeting to cut the 6.5 percent two-week repo rate, and may even cut market players' access to high-yield repos as Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did early this year to fend off speculation. \par \par \uc2  "They can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, they can cut interest rates, and they could also not sterilise surplus market liquidity with their repo tenders," said CSOB's Marek Gabris. \par \par \uc2  In Poland, April food price data due on Wednesday is expected to encourage a further reduction in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "The earlier fall of the zloty was caused purely by political risks, and now the markets return to fundamentals and focus on the EU referendum," said Jacek Wisniewski, analyst at Bank Pekao in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Zsigmond Jarai said on Tuesday he saw no reason for interest rate cuts, adding the NBH could only follow other cuts in the region if they prompted inflows into Hungary and lifted markets. \par \par \uc2  "Hungary can potentially deliver a cut if the forint were to strengthen to 240-242 (from around 245 to the euro)," said Chow. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030506dz5600rsh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
