\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Hungary int'l reserves drop to 13.05 bln euros. \b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 10:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, March 7 (Reuters) - The National Bank of Hungary (NBH) said on Friday that its international reserves dropped to 13.05 billion euros by the end of February from 14.19 billion euros at the end of January. \par \par \uc2  The bank published the figures on its official website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mnb.hu/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.mnb.hu}}}\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The drop in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is an important indication about the actual amount of euros offloaded on the markets by the NBH since its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-January, when it had to fend off a massive foreign speculative attack against the strong end of the forint's 30 percent wide flotation band. \par \par \uc2  NBH Governor Zsigmond Jarai said last month the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves dropped to 12-13 billion euros in February, adding that this lower figure already included proceeds from a eurobond worth one billion euros issued earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  Jarai said two-thirds of the speculative capital that came in January, had already left Hungary's markets. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the data published on Friday showed there was still a significant amount of 'hot forints' in the markets, seeking a home. \par \par \uc2  "These data suggest that the problem is still there...that there is still some 2.8 billion euros worth of hot money around," said Istvan Zsoldos at CA IB in Budapest. \par \par \uc2  "It's not worrying but it shows that it's not quite true that two-thirds of the hot money has left," he added. \par \par \uc2  In January the NBH bought an estimated almost five billion euros during \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the limits of the forint's ERM-2 style trading bands. \par \par \uc2  Then the bank also slashed its key interest rates and imposed strict limits on its key two-week deposit facility, trying to force speculators' 'hot forints' to leave Hungary's financial markets, where the huge excess liquidity hindered their efficient \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Last month the NBH reversed these earlier emergency measures, hiking the overnight deposit rate by 200 basis points to 5.50 percent and bringing monetary policy back to normal. \par \par \uc2  The bank also abolished restrictions on its weekly two-week deposit tender, which currently yields 6.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The bank said these steps could be made as two-thirds of the speculative capital which came in January had already left Hungary's markets, via the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's euro selling auctions, \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  or deals with local exporters. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international reserves jumped to 14.19 billion euros by the end of January, from 9.92 billion at end of 2002 as a result of the speculative attack on the band. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030307dz3700jgb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
