\par\par \b \uc2  Poland, Slovakia May Gain From Hungary's Forint Upheaval \b0\par\par\uc2  By Margit Feher \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,075 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2003\par 14:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST -(Dow Jones)- Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil in Hungary recently, analysts and fund managers say they haven't lost faith in the region. \par \par \uc2  They say that while Hungary - which had been one of the top euro-zone convergence plays of late - has lost some of its luster, other countries, notably Poland and Slovakia, remain attractive spots for those looking to park money in Central and Eastern Europe. \par \par \uc2  And, they say, Hungary shouldn't be written off just because some speculators got burned by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action last week. \par \par \uc2  "The fundamentals and the outlook haven't changed for Central and Eastern Europe, despite a rise in risk premiums for emerging countries," said Gyorgy Barcza, economist at ING Bank. "Polish, Slovak and even the Hungarian markets (can still) offer appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and relatively high bond yields." \par \par \uc2  In Hungary and Poland, which are headed for European Union membership in 2004, long-term bonds are providing real yields of 13%-14%, Barcza added. \par \par \uc2  The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's success at defending the forint's trading band last week with massive rate cuts and new restrictions on two-week deposits sent shock waves through markets, serving as a rude awakening to those who saw unlimited profits in the region's markets. \par \par \uc2  "Many leveraged speculators got severely burned (in Hungary)," said Callum Henderson, head of emerging EMEA research and strategy at Bank of America in London. "Until now, there was good money in Hungary." \par \par \uc2  Many managers responded by exiting Hungary and by winding up positions in other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In knee-jerk reactions, speculators estimated to have lost EUR300 million on the forint attack closed positions elsewhere, which hit the Polish zloty, and the Czech and Slovak korunas. \par \par \uc2  "After last week, a number of investors are probably attaching a somewhat higher risk premium to (regional) exposure," said in a research note Thursday. "(However,) the normal thing would be to assume that this quickly unwinds." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Dedicated Emerging Market Funds To Remain In Region\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts agree that any real worries should be short term. They point\par out that many dedicated emerging market funds see this area as the best\par choice among global emerging economies, especially when viewed against\par those in troubled Latin American countries.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Poland, the largest economy in the region, appears to be a top choice\par for investors. Polish "inflation remains remarkably benign, and real\par interest rates remain the highest in the region," Bank of America's\par Henderson said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This (recent zloty weakness) is a classic case of regional contagion,\par but the volatility's mainly technical," said Thomas Browne, vice\par president for emerging market research at Deutsche Bank in London. "I\par still believe we're in for a multiyear linear appreciation trend for the\par zloty,"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That trend could see the zloty at 3.60 against the euro by year-end, a\par 15% appreciation, Browne said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Slovakia, once the poor cousin of the Czech Republic, had been largely\par overlooked by investors since its split with Czechoslovakia. Now,\par however, prospects for a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - boosted by foreign direct\par capital inflows and high interest rates compared with neighbor Hungary -\par are helping to lure fund managers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Slovakia is very interesting," ING's Barcza said. "It has the most\par undervalued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Its economy is on the way to restructure to a\par market economy, and its productivity gains will lead to a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par appreciation, while inflation is to remain low."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Barcza said he expects the Slovak koruna to strengthen to SKK40.5\par against the euro by year-end from SKK42.35.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We have decided to add to our allocation (in Slovak bonds) despite\par underperformance year-to-date," said Bank of America's Henderson, who\par raised Slovakia's weight to 10% from 5% in his model portfolio. He said\par he favors the 7.8% bonds maturing January 2007 and the 9.00% bonds of\par April 2005.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        Russia Might Also Gain From Hungary's Woes\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other winners of capital flows out of Hungary could be Russia and\par Turkey, although Turkey's potential may be limited because of doubts\par about reform and geopolitical worries over a U.S. war with Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Russia has the highest weight in my portfolio," said Zoltan Cseko,\par fund manager at CIB Fund Manager in Budapest. "High oil prices are\par bond-negative, but for Russia, they provide fast economic growth."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Turkey is still in negotiations with the E.U. on getting a date to\par open formal negotiations for membership, and investors remain cautious\par despite its potential.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Turkish bond markets are neither a convincing nor cheap enough\par proposition to constitute an obvious convergence trade," UBS Warburg\par said in a research note. "But dedicated convergence funds are itching\par for new product. In this sense, 2003 will be the make or break year for\par Turkey as a convergence trade."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many analysts are quick to remind, though, that all is not lost in\par Hungary, saying that last week's 200-basis-point rate cut weakened the\par forint to an unsustainable level given \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  inflation goals.\par That means the bank eventually will have to hike rates to push the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  close to the strong edge of its trading band.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's success last week at defending its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band, some analysts speculate that Hungary could be pushed to\par eventually abandon it.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Likely E.U accession in May 2004 suggests that fundamental pressure\par for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation remains intact," said J.P. Morgan, the bank\par that is suspected of being behind the attack on the forint.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Most likely, local authorities eventually will revalue the forint's\par parity or widen the fluctuation band or let the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  float freely,"\par it added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bank allows the forint to trade in a plus-or-minus 15% band around\par a midpoint set against the euro at HUF276.11 ($1=HUF231.45). Foreign\par speculators, reportedly led by J.P. Morgan and Goldman Sachs, pushed the\par forint out of its band on the strong side at HUF234.69 in an effort to\par force Hungary to revalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading band.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by selling forints worth an\par estimated EUR5 billion to EUR7 billion over two days to weaken the\par forint. The bank slashed its key, two-week deposit rate to 6.5% and\par limited the amount for accepting deposits from commercial banks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Margit Feher, Dow Jones Newswires; +361-267-0622;\par margit.feher@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (David McQuaid in Warsaw contributed to this story.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030124dz1o000w1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
