\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary grain exports slow despite weak  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Denes Albert \par \uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2003\par 17:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Jan 20 (Reuters) - The weakening of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forint last week after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sharp interest rate cuts failed to boost grain exports, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to lose more ground to boost grain sales, traders said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Hungary's farmers have supplies of half a million tonnes of exportable wheat and at least twice as much maize, but demand was slow despite the weaker forint, they said. \par \par \uc2  "What we need is an even stronger dollar or higher international grains prices, but there's little immediate hope of either," said Robert Vajo, trader at brokerage Agrokont. \par \par \uc2  Last week's turmoil on Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, left key interest rates 200 basis points lower at 6.50 percent, and weakened the forint to 230 to the dollar from 220. \par \par \uc2  The weakening forint was followed by a rise in grain futures prices of 600-1,000 forints per tonne, dampening potential export demand. \par \par \uc2  "Wheat is trickling out of the country to neighbouring destinations, but only in tens of thousands of tonnes at a time," said Janos Szojka, a trader at brokerage Swap. \par \par \uc2  Hungarian grains prices need to be reduced quite sharply to compete with cheaper alternative sources in the Ukraine and Russia, he said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month, the Jordanian government said it bought 100,000 tonnes of Russian, Kazakh and Hungarian origin hard wheat. \par \par \uc2  "Our only hope is that grains from the East will not have duty-free access to the European Union this year," Szojka said. \par \par \uc2  Agrokont's Vajo said a few truckloads of wheat found regular buyers in Romania and Yugoslavia but beyond that there was little in the way of new deals. \par \par \uc2  Szojka added that with hundreds of thousands of tonnes of grain in state-subsidised stores there was little pressure on farmers to go below current asking prices. \par \par \uc2  He added that grains returning from storage in the spring may not make too much difference either. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the Budapest Commodity Exchange, nearby March maize  closed at 21,990\par forints ($92.58) per tonne on Monday, down 210 forints, May      lost 270\par forints to 22,050 and July         fell 300 forints to 22,850.\par March wheat  eased 70 forints to 22,580, but May          and August    were\par unchanged at 22,750 and 23,000 forints respectively.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030120dz1k00w4s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
