\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Poland's euro ambitions hinge on budget reform. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wojciech Moskwa \par \uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2003\par 15:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Jan 17 (Reuters) - Poland's goal of adopting the euro by its target date of 2007 will slip out of reach if the government fails to tackle budget reforms now to boost investment and cut welfare handouts, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But deep reform is unlikely given the government's weak economic record and its fear that unveiling spending cuts before a European Union referendum in June could hurt the "Yes" vote. \par \par \uc2  Recent ministerial changes, including Friday's sacking of Health Minister Mariusz Lapinski, risk further disruption to economic reforms already running behind schedule. \par \par \uc2  Poland needs to fix the budget so that it can absorb billions of euros in development funds from Brussels after the eastern European country's expected EU accession in May 2004. Such funds require co-financing by the beneficiary state. \par \par \uc2  The region's other main EU hopefuls, Hungary and the Czech Republic, are also struggling to bring their budget deficits below a ceiling of three percent of gross domestic product required by the Maastricht Treaty to qualify for the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Grzegorz Kolodko had promised to present details of his budget reforms in the New Year but has still not revealed details, leaving the issue on the back burner for now. \par \par \uc2  But Jacek Tomkiewicz, an economist at Kolodko's "Tiger" think-tank and ministry aide, said some guidelines had been set. \par \par \uc2  "We will need to find an extra 10 billion zlotys ($2.6 billion) or so in our budget for EU-related costs. Our aim is to do this without increasing the deficit," Tomkiewicz said. \par \par \uc2  Poland has set its 2003 deficit at 39 billion zlotys, or 3.9 percent of GDP. Mandatory spending, such as index-linked rises in pensions, amount to nearly 70 percent of all outlays. \par \par \uc2  Changes could include a shakeup of the generous farmers' pension scheme, called KRUS, but that could run into heavy opposition from the Peasants Party, the junior partner in the ruling coalition. \par \par \uc2  "I don't expect a sweeping reform. Very few details will become public before the referendum," said Janusz Jankowiak, chief economist at BRE Bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  IF NOT 2007, THEN WHEN? \par \par \uc2  The 14-month-old left-wing cabinet has freed up Poland's rigid labour code but failed to restructure loss-making heavy industries like coal mining, railways and shipbuilding, fearful of adding to a record 18 percent jobless rate. \par \par \uc2  "If the 2004 budget is glued together by an increase in the deficit, then talk about joining the euro zone in 2007 is completely unrealistic," said Witold Orlowski, economic adviser to Polish President Aleksander Kwasniewski. \par \par \uc2  "Poland's price stability is not guaranteed and if we delay budget reforms and only meet fiscal entry conditions in five or six years, we may then find we don't meet other criteria." \par \par \uc2  Delayed euro entry would be a blow to convergence investors who have bet tens of billions of euros on early euro adoption and could be a disincentive for government reforms. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, keeping the zloty longer would help Poland maintain natural steam vents for its developing economy - enabling it, for instance to run a higher inflation rate and see its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate. \par \par \uc2  "Meeting the Maastricht Criteria should be the basis of policy-making for any government regardless of the accession schedule," said Tim Ash, economist at Bear Stearns in London. "I am not convinced it wouldn't be better for Poland's economy to develop further ahead of EMU membership." \par \par \uc2  BUDGETS AND \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  BANDS \par \par \uc2  This week's market meltdown in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and slashed interest rates to contain the forint, also highlights the risk of running a wide deficit while imposing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading band. \par \par \uc2  High deficits, financed by debt issues, have sparked foreign portfolio inflows and boosted \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the region. \par \par \uc2  So unless borrowing needs are lowered, Poland and the Czech Republic - running a deficit of 6.3 percent of GDP in 2003 - risk similar problems once they ditch their free floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the pre-euro Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM-2) band. \par \par \uc2  Boguslaw Grabowski, one of 10 members of Poland's rate-setting council, told Reuters joining the ERM's 30 percent trading band without a plan to fulfil the Maastricht criteria would be a "huge mistake". \par \par \uc2  The Czechs, who do not expect to adopt the euro before 2009, aim to curb their deficit to 3.7 percent of GDP in 2006 mostly by curbing welfare payments, sick leave and jobless benefits. \par \par \uc2  Hungary, whose inflation prospects are unclear following this week's fiasco, still eyes EMU membership in 2007 but faces a formidable challenge in reforming public finances first after the deficit topped eight percent of GDP last year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030117dz1h00r7b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
