\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 5-Hungary c.bank changes tack, steadies forint. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Krisztina Than and Sandor Peto \par \uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2003\par 19:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Jan 17 (Reuters) - Claiming to have beaten off speculative attacks that drove the forint to the top of its trading band this week, Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday offloaded some of the billions of euros it earlier bought to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  After a roller-coaster two days in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates by 200 basis points and bought 4-5 billion euros at 234.69 forints to defend its exchange rate regime, it reversed direction and sold euros at around 245 forints. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) was trying to keep markets on their toes, signalling it wanted the forint to firm within the band, but not enough to move it back to the top where it had given speculators a clear target to attack. \par \par \uc2  "I believe the bank is, and will be, selling batches of euros...trying to stabilise the forint in a...range which would be a more difficult target for speculators to hit," said Istvan Zsoldos at CA IB Securities. \par \par \uc2  The forint closed around 244.50/80 to the euro, or 11.4 percent above parity in its 30 percent wide band versus late Thursday levels nearer 250. \par \par \uc2  ING Bank's Gyorgy Barcza said the NBH was playing cat-and-mouse with the markets, propping up the forint - which dropped around six percent on Thursday in the wake of the bank's shock rate cuts and market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - while keeping markets guessing as to its real goal. \par \par \uc2  "The bank's primary goal now is to scare off speculators, but it also wants to take profits on its long euro positions worth up to five billion euros," Barcza said, adding he reckoned the NBH had offloaded 500-800 million euros. \par \par \uc2  Others put the NBH's Friday euro sales at much lower levels. \par \par \uc2  NO BAND SHIFT \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Csaba Laszlo said a concerted government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort had seen off the speculators. \par \par \uc2  He again ruled out any shift in the band for the next 18-24 months, by which time Hungary will be in the European Union and possibly inside the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM-2), the waiting room for euro zone members. \par \par \uc2  "It seems this (week's) speculative attack has not been successful and those who speculated on a forint band shift will suffer very big losses," Laszlo told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Traders, however, said they expected markets to stay choppy. \par \par \uc2  "The big amounts from the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are circulating, and looking for a home," said Peter Koppi at Raiffeisen Securities. \par \par \uc2  "There is uncertainty." \par \par \uc2  Bond dealers reported hectic trade, with prices gyrating as foreign investors sought to exit \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  after being burnt by the NBH's unpredictable \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate cut tactics. \par \par \uc2  "Selling was stronger, with foreigners fleeing the (bond) market," said Robert Barlai of Erste Bank Investment. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said foreign investors had only wound up a small chunk of the short euro positions built up earlier in the week when the NBH \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 234.69 to the euro to defend the top of the band. \par \par \uc2  "The massive short positions are still here," one said. \par \par \uc2  "The longer it takes for the forint to go back above 240, the more investors will have to ask themselves how much loss they're ready to take," the dealer added. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Bank trader Pal Geszti said the forint would stay between 240 and 250 to the euro for the next couple of weeks. \par \par \uc2  "Those holding short positions must bid...we expect them to step in if the forint rises above 240 to try and get out with as little a loss as possible," he said. \par \par \uc2  ROLLER-COASTER \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday, the NBH cut key interest rates by 100 basis points for the\par second time in two days to 6.5 percent and lowered the overnight\par deposit rate to 3.5 percent to depress the forint            from the\par top of the band.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The band, designed to steady the forint, was widened in May 2001 as\par part of Hungary's preparation for euro zone entry, possibly by 2007.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hungary is central Europe's only EU candidate to have already adopted a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime similar to the ERM.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The forint's roller-coaster ride knocked some of the shine off the\par Slovak crown         and the Polish zloty                as investors\par closed positions in the wake of Hungary's monetary mayhem.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030117dz1h00mdq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
