\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary Eurobond Issue Plans Could Be Scaled Back-Agency \b0\par\par\uc2 246 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 19:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST -(Dow Jones)- Hungary may scale back its Eurobond issue plans for this year if the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market steadily to defend the forint's trading band, state news agency MTI quoted Minister of Finance Csaba Laszlo as saying Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  continuously, and that pumps up the bank's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, we may reconsider the issue of a further EUR1 billion in Eurobonds," Laszlo was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  Hungary said earlier this week that it plans to issue EUR1 billion in eurobonds, to be followed by other issues totaling a further EUR1 billion later this year. \par \par \uc2  But Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the forint market to weaken the forint, as obliged legally when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits its strongest spot in its trading band. The bank defended the forint's trading band by selling forints for euros. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the bank sold forints worth several hundreds of millions of euros in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The bank allows the forint to trade in a plus or minus 15% band with the band's midpoint fixed against the euro at HUF276.11. The strongest level of the forint against the euro is at HUF234.69, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to accept euros for forints. \par \par \uc2  -By Margit Feher, Dow Jones Newswires; +361-267-0622; margit.feher@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030115dz1f0015f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
