\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Hungary cenbank chief sees no room to cut rates. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Louis Charbonneau \par \uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 12:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  VIENNA, Jan 15 (Reuters) - Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Wednesday that he saw no opportunity for interest rates cuts at present and insisted that a relatively strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was needed to achieve low inflation. \par \par \uc2  Zsigmond Jarai was speaking to an investment conference in Vienna just minutes before the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop the forint breaching the upper limits of its trading band. \par \par \uc2  He said he agreed with the government that there was no need at the moment to adjust the band. \par \par \uc2  "We don't see any room (for rate cuts) at the moment," Jarai said in answer to a question why Hungarian interest rates have not been slashed to weaken the strong forint. \par \par \uc2  "Why we didn't decrease the interest rate this week? The reason was heavy inflationary pressure in the economy," he added. \par \par \uc2  The NBH has come under pressure to cut rates from the strengthening forint, which the government says hurts exporters but Jarai said a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to control inflation. \par \par \uc2  "We do believe that to achieve low inflation we need a relatively strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Jarai added. \par \par \uc2  A Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman in Budapest said on Wednesday the bank had stepped into the market to buy euros from domestic banks when the forint reached the 234.69 per euro strong end of its 30 percent trading band. \par \par \uc2  BUDGET DEFICIT \par \par \uc2  Jarai said that given the country's ballooning budget deficit there was no way the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could risk a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We would be happy to achieve low inflation with a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the deficit would be substantially lower and wage increases would be controlled by an income policy," he said. \par \par \uc2  He noted that the budget deficit had swollen from 4.1 percent of gross domestic product in 2001 to "nearly 10 percent" last year. While the official figure was 9.4 percent for 2002, he said it would soon be revised. \par \par \uc2  "The only good macroeconomic indicator (in Hungary) is the relatively low inflation," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said that if wage growth was under control and the budget deficit slashed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "might find the room to loosen monetary conditions". \par \par \uc2  "We fully agree with the government that there is no need to change the band," he said. \par \par \uc2  Regarding the strengthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jarai said Hungary's expected European Union entry in 2004 was playing a role in market sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment there appears to be euphoria in the market due to EU accession," he said. But he warned that a shift in sentiment in the opposite direction could easily come as a result of some unforseen event or development. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030115dz1f00m8g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
