\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Forint tests band, c.bank says not  \highlight22\uc2 intervened \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2003\par 16:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BUDAPEST, Jan 13 (Reuters) - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's forint         tested its\par trading band for the first time on Monday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (NBH)\par disappointed some in the market by leaving key interest rates on hold.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , buoyed recently by keen foreign demand\par chasing high yields ahead of future euro zone entry, traded at 234.69\par to the euro, a maximum 15 percent above the band's central rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "There's been a deal at 234.69 versus the euro in electronic trade," said Attila Behan at K&H Bank. \par \par \uc2  Other dealers confirmed the forint hit the top of its band for the first time since the trading range was widened to 15 percent either side of parity in May 2001. \par \par \uc2  At that level, the NBH has to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying euros for forints to defend the regime. \par \par \uc2  But a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman told Reuters the bank had not sold forints on the market. \par \par \uc2  "(Market players) are trading among themselves," Gabor Missura said. "We're open to 3 p.m. (1400 GMT) and, until then, no one came to us to buy forints." \par \par \uc2  NO RATE CUT, NO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  NBH Governor Zsigmond Jarai said recently the bank would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market before the forint hit the top of its band, but once there, it would defend the regime. \par \par \uc2  Another possible way to ease forint pressure is to trim interest rates but, despite some expectations for a 50 basis point cut on Monday, the NBH opted to keep rates on hold at 8.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the bank was keen to show its policy continued to focus on cutting inflation. Hungary is due to join the European Union in May 2004, and could enter the ERM-2, the euro zone waiting room, around the same time. It is the only central European EU candidate to have already adopted an ERM-type \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. \par \par \uc2  The strong forint has hit exporters, who traditionally drive the country's economic growth, and who are already reeling as main markets like Germany are in the doldrums. \par \par \uc2  The forint's rise highlights the bank's dilemma on how to defend its 4.5 percent end-2003 inflation target with a tight monetary policy when it can only curb the forint by cutting interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Hungary's inflation, at around five percent, is also one of the highest among the pre-EU states. The NBH wants to bring down inflation to 2-3 percent next year in readiness for euro zone entry, possibly by 2007. \par \par \uc2  During recent forint rallies, analysts said speculative inflows could force a revaluation of the band's parity rate. \par \par \uc2  But both the centre-left government and the independent NBH have said the band would not be revalued before Hungary joins the ERM-2. \par \par \uc2  The cabinet opposes revaluation as this would further dent export competitiveness. It would prefer to see lower interest rates, temporarily sacrificing the NBH's tight inflation target. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030113dz1d00upo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
