\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Hungary c.bank baffles markets, wins time. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sandor Peto \par \uc2 709 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2002\par 17:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Nov 26 (Reuters) - Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) has bought time by sowing confusion in the markets amid fevered speculation over the strong forint and possible interest rate cuts, analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the bank took markets by surprise by lopping half a percentage point off key rates - changing tack on a long-standing policy of monetary tightening due to inflation pressures from a soaring budget deficit and surging wages. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, it surprised markets again - leaving rates unchanged amid wide expectations of a cut of anything between 25 and 100 basis points. \par \par \uc2  All the while, the forint has continued to gain, hitting new highs on Tuesday at around 235.60 against the euro, triggering complaints from exporters. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength is due to heavy capital inflows on the prospects of a smooth European Union entry in 2004 and a fall in interest rates to lower euro zone levels. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Monday's non-move on rates probably cost a lot of market players a lot of money, showing Hungary's markets are not yet just a one-way bet. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's decision leads us to believe that causing confusion could be the bank's strategy," said Mojmir Hampl, analyst at Ceska Sporitelna in Prague. \par \par \uc2  Hampl said a rate cut this week would merely have triggered expectations for further reductions. \par \par \uc2  "By leaving rates steady, the bank proved to be an active player, leaving the ball in the market's court," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said the market was split between those continuing to bet on the bank eventually giving in and cutting rates, and those who were increasingly nervous that the bank may hold firm. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  TOOL \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rates are still the highest in pre-EU Central Europe, and bearish stock markets and alternative emerging markets have helped sustain keen interest in the region's assets, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Hungary's five-year government bonds traded at yields around seven percent on Tuesday, compared with 5.8 percent on the corresponding Polish and 3.8 percent on German five-year bonds. \par \par \uc2  By 1530 GMT, the forint was back down at around 236.20 to the euro, still near the 234.69 top of its trading band, enough to keep speculation alive that Hungary may have to revalue the band's parity upwards in the months ahead. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the NBH had bought time with its two surprise rate announcements, and had not yet used all the tools it has to discourage speculation if investors get too confident. \par \par \uc2  The risk of the bank using these tools - primarily direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - now hangs over investors, they said, adding that with no rate cut this week the NBH has more room to cut if needed. \par \par \uc2  The head of Hungary's second biggest commercial bank, K&H, earlier said the NBH could curb the forint by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sending a signal that the forint can move into reverse, too. \par \par \uc2  Other central banks in the region, like Slovakia, have used direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to win time, analysts added. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is useful not because it can cause a temporary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  easing...but it can cause losses and make speculators uncertain," said Dirk Woelfer at Standard & Poor's MMS in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "(Both sides) are now testing each other and contemplating how far the other side is ready to go," he added. \par \par \uc2  Current Hungarian yields offer investors lucrative returns, but some analysts warned it could be difficult to sustain recent gains in central Europe and there could be a hard landing. \par \par \uc2  In Hungary, concerns over a high budget deficit and inflation may resurface, in addition to the risks involved in trying to fathom \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, they said. \par \par \uc2  ""For safety reasons, we recommend not raising Hungarian exposure," said Ceska Sporitelna's Hampl said. "Short-term investors should think about profit-taking." \par \par \uc2  Others, however, said convergence play would continue and the NBH would finally surrender to massive inflows. \par \par \uc2  "(The NBH) will almost certainly cut rates in two weeks time (to spur growth)," HSBC said in a regional report. \par \par \uc2  "...some time next year, it will have to choose between watering down or abandoning its inflation targeting..., or revaluing central parity to allow further forint appreciation." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021126dybq00x0x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
