\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -E.Europe units rally as Irish approve Nice. \b0\par\par\uc2 927 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2002\par 11:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 21 (Reuters) - The main emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied strongly on Monday after Ireland's approval of the Nice Treaty on European Union enlargement at the weekend removed a major obstacle standing in the way of the process. \par \par \uc2  The Irish approved the treaty by a convincing margin, with 63 percent voting in favour to 37 percent against, prompting a collective sigh of relief from the 10 candidate countries hoping to be admitted to the EU in 2004. \par \par \uc2  The Czech crown, Hungarian forint and Polish zloty all jumped when local trade resumed on Monday and analysts said the rally could continue over the next few sessions. \par \par \uc2  "The rally might extend over the next couple of days," said Koon Chow, central Europe \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CSFB. \par \par \uc2  "Even though there was anticipation of a 'yes' vote, investors had been holding hedging positions in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the interest rate market, because they thought a 'no' result would trigger a more violent reaction." \par \par \uc2  Emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets have been seeing strong inflows for much of this year as investors bet on their economies converging to EU standards ahead of EU and euro entry. \par \par \uc2  Chow added that distinction should be made between the different countries, with domestic economic fundamentals now likely to come back into focus. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally is likely to have the most longevity in Poland, where domestic fundamentals are the most persuasive. \par \par \uc2  "In the Czech Republic, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still stands against appreciation with rate cuts and direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  there are still question marks over the direction of the twin deficits." \par \par \uc2  At 0930 GMT, the zloty traded up 0.48 percent against the euro from late Friday at 4.03, the forint was up 0.75 percent at 242.98, while the crown was 0.2 percent firmer at 3.48. \par \par \uc2  FOCUS TURNS TO BRUSSELS \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said a more cautious tone could return to the market ahead of the Brussels summit on enlargement later this week. \par \par \uc2  The meeting is set to discuss the terms of accession for the candidate countries and the negotiations are likely to be tough, particularly on the contentious issue of agricultural subsidies. \par \par \uc2  There are also worries about the stance of the Dutch after their centre-right government collapsed last week, in part over disagreements on the enlargement issue. \par \par \uc2  Two junior partners in the coalition opposed admitting Poland, Slovakia and Cyprus to the EU - though constitutional experts have stressed that the caretaker government has the legal authority to approve enlargement at the Brussels meeting. \par \par \uc2  "(The summit) would normally be seen as a formality, but instead could prove the venue for some serious wrangling, as the Dutch and other EU governments play hardball over accession terms," said analysts at Credit Agricole Indosuez, in a daily research note. \par \par \uc2  POLL BOOST FOR HUNGARIAN GOVERNMENT \par \par \uc2  The forint made the most headway of the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, getting an additional lift from the strong showing of the ruling centre-left coalition at Hungary's weekend local elections. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Peter Medgyessy's Socialists and their liberal Free Democrat coalition allies won a majority of 17 out of 19 counties in Sunday's vote, according to early results. \par \par \uc2  The result should strengthen Medgyessy's hand in his efforts to rein in Hungary's twin budget and current account deficits. \par \par \uc2  "We now need to see evidence of a fall in the budget deficit - the weekend local elections have cleared a major political obstacle for the government, making it more confident to cut back spending," said Chow at CSFB. \par \par \uc2  The Israeli shekel continued its recent strong run, rising over half a percent to fresh one-month highs around 4.7260. \par \par \uc2  A strong rebound in U.S. technology stocks has powered the rally, with many Israeli tech firms quoted on the U.S. Nasdaq stock index. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq rallied around six percent last week after some encouraging signs on the corporate earnings front. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                 LATEST    PREV   LOCAL    LOCAL\par                                    CLOSE  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                           PCT CHG  PCT CHG\par                                                    IN 2002\par  EUROPE/MIDDLE EAST/AFRICA\par  Czech Rep.               30.48     30.55   +0.21    +3.98\par  Poland                    4.04      4.05   +0.40   -12.74\par  Hungary                 242.78    244.80   +0.83    +0.67\par  Slovakia                 41.58     41.77   +0.45    +2.74\par  Romania                 33,280    33,310   +0.09    -4.63\par  Ukraine                   5.33      5.33   +0.00    +0.21\par  South Africa             10.33     10.35   +0.15   +15.86\par  Kenya                    79.20     79.30   +0.13    -0.95\par  Israel                    4.73      4.75   +0.53    -6.79\par  Turkey               1,663,000 1,655,000   -0.48   -13.41\par  \par  LATAM\par  Mexico           