\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-Forint seen falling ahead of election, surging later. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sandor Peto \par \uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2002\par 10:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Feb 25 (Reuters) - The forint is seen falling against the euro ahead of Hungary's general election in April, but then rebounding and nearing the top of its flotation band by next year, a monthly Reuters poll of analysts showed on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The consensus in the survey , conducted between February 20 and 22 with 15 participants, sees a fall in the forint exchange rate against the euro to 245.25 by the end of March from Friday's close at 243.60/86. \par \par \uc2  The most pessimistic analyst projected a slump to 252.00. \par \par \uc2  Hungary will hold two rounds of elections on April 7 and 21. \par \par \uc2  PRE-ELECTION FALL SEEN TEMPORARY \par \par \uc2  The main election rivals - ruling centre-right Fidesz-MPP and opposition Socialists - are neck-to-neck in opinion polls. \par \par \uc2  Both are emphasising Hungary's bid to join the European Union (EU) in 2004, but analysts are worried that Fidesz may be corralled into a coalition with the extreme right MIEP party. \par \par \uc2  Analysts' main scenario is that the forint quietens after the vote and economic fundamentals fuel a rise. Some analysts even say the rise may lead Hungary to revaluing the parity rate in the 30-percent-wide forint flotation band. \par \par \uc2  The forint, one of the main Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen benefitting from convergence with the EU, came into the limelight earlier this month after rises to record highs. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged to 239.60 against the euro after Prime Minister Viktor Orban projected a 3-5 percent rise every year in the run-up to euro zone membership, expected for 2006 or 2007. \par \par \uc2  The forint retreated last week after a sharp upwards revision in the 2001 current account deficit, verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) and a 50 basis point cut in the NBH's two-week deposit rate to 8.50 percent last Monday. \par \par \uc2  PARITY CHANGE A RISK \par \par \uc2  According to analysts' consensus the forint is expected to firm to 242.37 by the end of this year and to 238.63 by the end of 2003. The average forecasts were slashed by 1.66 and 3.21 forints, respectively, after Orban's comments on the forint. \par \par \uc2  The consensus projects softer levels than the top of the 30-percent-wide forint band at 234.69, but most analysts said an upwards revaluation of the parity rate is likely by the end of 2003 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to avoid speculative attacks. \par \par \uc2  Two of 11 analysts projected an upwards 10 percentage point parity rate revaluation for the second half of this year, five forecast a change for 2003, two said no revaluation was expected this year, while two said a change would not come before 2004. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the revaluation risk rose after the prime minister's comments. \par \par \uc2  "We expect no modification...this year," Lehman Brothers said in its weekly outlook for Hungarian markets. "However, Pandora's box was opened, and from now on the market may start to price in some chance of an early parity appreciation." \par \par \uc2  - If you are interested in more on Hungary's elections, please visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.hu/elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.hu/elections}}}\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020225dy2p00gjk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
