\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Hungary Can Live With Strong Forint - Jarai \b0\par\par\uc2  By Margit Feher \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2002\par 17:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST -(Dow Jones)- Hungary's strong forint, which has soared under pressure from strong investment inflows, poses no threat to growth, said National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Zsigmond Jarai Friday. \par \par \uc2  Hence, he said, no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market or in the managed exchange rate regime is needed, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength supports the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2002 and 2003 inflation goals. \par \par \uc2  But any further rise is not needed to meet inflation targets, he added. \par \par \uc2  "With the current 243-248 forint level (versus the euro), we can comfortably meet our inflation targets. We don't target an exchange rate. Further firming is not necessary. We don't want to undershoot the 2001 inflation target, but it doesn't require any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Jarai said. \par \par \uc2  The bank seeks to lower annual inflation to 4.2-4.5% in December 2002 and 3.5% in December 2003 from an expected 7% or thereabouts in December 2001, he said. \par \par \uc2  "There's room to reduce rates if the exchange rate remains in the current range and inflation falls," Jarai added. The last time the bank cut its key rate was on Monday, by 25 basis points to 9.5%. \par \par \uc2  Jarai made his comments in response to market speculation that the recent firming of the forint against the euro would prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to talk down the forint, or even change the current managed exchange rate mechanism, which limits the forint's trading range. \par \par \uc2  But the system will remain in place until 2004, the date Hungary plans to join the European Union, Jarai said. \par \par \uc2  "We'll need to renegotiate the regime with the European Union, and then we'll need to readjust the (forint's central) parity, but we won't change the fluctuation band," Jarai said, talking to a small group of journalists after a press conference held jointly with government ministers on Hungary's economic outlook for 2002. \par \par \uc2  The forint's central parity is set at HUF276.105 around which the bank allows a variation of plus or minus 15%. \par \par \uc2  The forint had firmed to over 12% on the strong side of its parity prior to Monday's rate cut and also Thursday on continued strong portfolio receipts chasing interest rate carry trades on Hungarian government bonds over the euro. Hungary's sound economic standing and convergence prospects are also sucking money in. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                EU Compliance Drives Policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Friday afternoon, the forint regained its early-session losses and\par trading calmed down, following the Polish zloty's recovery from a steep\par fall and Jarai's comments.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At around 1500 GMT it was trading at HUF244.37 against the euro, much\par firmer from HUF248 early Friday and slightly weaker than late Thursday's\par HUF244.00-30.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As to the outlook for 2002, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and government will press\par ahead with their current fiscal and monetary policies in pursuit of\par ultimate compliance with E.U. requirements.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We want to launch the euro in 2006," Jarai reiterated Friday. "Our\par fiscal, monetary and overall economic policy targets take that into\par account first."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In spring 2001, the bank widened the forint's band from +/-2.25% in\par preparation for joining the E.U.'s Exchange Rate Mechanism II. It also\par launched an inflation-targeting policy because Hungary's number one task\par when joining the euro zone is to lower inflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With a wider band, the forint strengthened last year to HUF246 against\par the euro from HUF265 at the start of 2001.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, productivity gains have offset the negative impact of the\par strong forint on exports, which are driving the country's growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The stronger forint has been forcing small and medium-size firms - the\par hardest hit by the stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - to modernize, Minister of\par Economic Affairs Gyorgy Matolcsy said Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gross domestic product is expected to rise between 3.5-4% this year,\par somewhat slower than around 4% in 2001, Minister of Finance Mihaly Varga\par said at the press conference.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The current account deficit, which the bank and the government expect\par below EUR1 billion in 2001, will widen to between EUR1.5-2 billion in\par 2002, both benign figures, reflecting how strongly Hungary can weather\par the global economic slowdown, they said. The deficit totaled EUR1.62\par billion in 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Margit Feher, Dow Jones Newswires; +361-267-0622;\par margit.feher@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020111dy1b000z1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
