\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary govt, c.bank expected to talk forint down. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sandor Peto \par \uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2002\par 16:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Jan 10 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally on Friday to try and curb a rising forint after substantial capital inflows in recent weeks, analysts and dealers said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  National Bank of Hungary Governor Zsigmond Jarai, Finance Minister Mihaly Varga and Economics Minister Gyorgy Matolcsy are due to hold a joint news conference on Friday (1000 GMT) on the economic prospects this year. \par \par \uc2  "We don't think (exchange rate) measures will be announced, but they will try to talk (the forint) down," said Istvan Gere, dealer at K&H Bank. "This is is a trend in Central Europe, the Polish and Czech situation is also similar." \par \par \uc2  Varga told reporters on Thursday that "foreign observers" currently see the forint, the Czech crown and Polish zloty as strong. \par \par \uc2  "The strong forint obviously has an impact on the economy. We will have talks with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economics Minister on Friday and certainly we will also speak about that," he said. \par \par \uc2  Answering a question, Varga did not rule out officials talking about the forint at Friday's news conference. \par \par \uc2  The forint has firmed to below 244 against the euro since early last month when it was more than than 250. \par \par \uc2  Government bond yields have fallen sharply on fresh capital inflows, with the amount held by foreign investors rising some 100 billion forints ($366.8 million) to above 1,100 billion. \par \par \uc2  The forint is near its all-time high of 241 to the euro hit last July and is just three percentage points below the top of its 30-percent-wide flotation band. \par \par \uc2  CONVERGING REGION, SURGING \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  The forint's surge fits a regional trend fuelled by falling inflation and relatively high fixed income yields as several states get closer to European Union membership, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Hungary and other countries in the region hope to join the EU in 2004 and enter the euro zone two or three years later. \par \par \uc2  "Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause a headache for policy makers in the CEE countries as the generally unfavourable external environment mixed with strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits export opportunities," said Zoltan Torok, analyst at Raiffeisen Securities. \par \par \uc2  The Polish and Czech governments are also considering measures to curb their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid strong portfolio capital and foreign direct investment inflows. \par \par \uc2  NEW HUNGARY REGIME SEEN STANDING PRESSURE \par \par \uc2  Hungary widened the forint's 4.5-percent band to 30 percent last May as part of a series of moves to prepare the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM II), the euro anteroom. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the new broader band was likely to withstand the pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Before ERM II, an exchange rate adjustment will be needed anyway, but an adjustment two years before ERM would make no sense," said Marton Nagy of ING Investment Management. \par \par \uc2  "If the forint reaches 13-14 percent above parity in the band, many investors will start to think the top is too close and begin to liquidate positions," he said. \par \par \uc2  Other analysts did not rule out the chance that capital inflows could push the forint near to the top of the band, but they said verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate cuts, widely expected in the market, were sufficient to contain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. \par \par \uc2  "The 25 basis point rate cut (on Monday) was just a signal that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is uncomfortable with the forint so strong," Nagy said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020110dy1a00v0r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
