\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary forint eases as global uncertainty weighs. \b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2001\par 16:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Sept 20 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint closed weaker at around 6.20 percent above parity in its trading band on Thursday and traders said if foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases continue, the forint might fall even further. \par \par \uc2  "We saw continuous foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys today, though not heavy buys," said Orsolya Liptai at K&H Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The 259 (forints to the euro) level has supported the forint so far, but if foreigners keep buying (euro) we can go above 260," she added. Liptai said at such levels the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) should already consider a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The forint closed on Wednesday at 6.50 percent above midline in its 30-percent-wide trading band. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro the forint closed at 258.70/259.20, easing from Wednesday's close at 258.00/30. \par \par \uc2  That is well below the 245-250 level which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said was needed to reach inflation targets of 6-8 percent for this year and 3.5-5.5 percent for 2002. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said on Thursday that the NBH should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least verbally to support the forint if global market uncertainty continues to weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do something in the short term, at least verbally," Lehman Brothers analyst Gabor Bekes told a news conference. "There is a risk that investors begin to doubt that the NBH is ready to act for a stronger forint." \par \par \uc2  A likely retaliation by the U.S. may make the forint fragile as investors may avoid emerging markets amid global uncertainty, regardless of Hungary's strong fundamentals and its convergence with the European Union, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Versus the dollar the forint ended at 278.70/279.30, compared with 278.90/279.20 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The forint floats in a 15 percent-wide range on either side of a parity rate set daily by the National Bank of Hungary against a euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. From April 1, the forint has been devalued every working day at a 0.2 percent monthly rate. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will terminate this crawling peg devaluation as of October 1. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010921dx9k009ax\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
