\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary c.bank should support forint, analysts say. \b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2001\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Sept 20 (Reuters) - The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least verbally to support the forint if global market uncertainty continues to weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "We expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do something in the short term, at least verbally," Lehman Brothers analyst Gabor Bekes told a news conference. "There is a risk that investors begin to doubt that the NBH is ready to act for a stronger forint." \par \par \uc2  The unit was quoted at 258.10/50 against the euro at 1310 /1110 GMT, down from its close at 252.25/50 last Monday before the attacks on the U.S.. \par \par \uc2  The forint was well below the 245-250 level which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it needed to attain to reach its inflation targets of 6-8 percent for this year and 3.5-5.5 percent for next. \par \par \uc2  Bekes said Hungary could achieve the targets even if the forint was slightly weaker than planned as a slowdown in the global economy was expected to cut import prices. \par \par \uc2  However, a likely retaliation by the U.S. may make the forint fragile as investors may avoid emerging markets amid global uncertainty, regardless of Hungary's strong fundamentals and its convergence with the European Union, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "For the time being what I can see is that the forint exhange rate is falling and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not take moves to strengthen confidence," Bekes said, adding that the NBH should communicate its preferences concerning the unit. \par \par \uc2  Zoltan Torok of Raiffeisen Securities said the NBH should not cut its interest rates in the short term as the current risk premium was part of the forint's defence against further falls. \par \par \uc2  "Our fear is that even if we do not see a large selling wave selling pressure will weigh on us," said Zoltan Torok. "In a situation like this an early rate cut would not be responsible behaviour." \par \par \uc2  Other analysts said the NBH may still reduce its interest rates at the next meeting of its Monetary Council scheduled for Monday after rate cuts in the U.S. and Europe. \par \par \uc2  "Beside the current state of the forint the inflation target is still reachable," said Marton Nagy of ING Investment Management. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010920dx9k00jas\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
