\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary forint firms to 11.65 pct on bond buying. \b0\par\par\uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2001\par 15:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, July 2 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint strengthened to close at 11.65 percent above parity on Monday, despite expectations in early trade that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would ease on a lack of market-moving news, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  After opening steady from its close on Friday, at 11.1 percent above midline, a flurry of buying in government bonds coupled with a National Bank of Hungary bond auction pushed the forint higher in its flotation band, said Attila Behan, dealer at K&H. \par \par \uc2  "After weakening this morning there were signs it would weaken further - but suddenly the picture just changed at midday," he said. "It was (due to) trading in Hungarian bonds." \par \par \uc2  Behan said the forint could easily rise to 12 percent above midline in its band in the rest of the week. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at 242.60/90, firming from Friday's close of 243.20/80. Versus the dollar it closed at 287.10/50, from 287.40/90. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated in profit-taking on Friday, after rises to record highs at above 11.7 percent last week despite warnings from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the forint was too strong. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if the forint reaches the top of the band. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has firmed over nine percent within the band since May 4 when the band was widened to 15 percent either side of a daily-set central parity rate, from 2.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  The forint floats in a 15-percent-wide range on either side of a parity rate set daily by the National Bank of Hungary against a euro curency basket. From April 1, 2001, the forint is devalued every working day at a 0.2 percent monthly rate. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010912dx7200c9l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
