\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary cbank seen acting soon to harness forint. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Krisztina Than \par \uc2 823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2001\par 16:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, June 26 (Reuters) - The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop the forint strengthening further if verbal warnings do not do the trick, analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The forint has firmed steadily since the National Bank of Hungary (NBH) overhauled its monetary regime in May by widening the forint trading band, making it fully convertible, and launching an inflation-targeting policy. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday NBH general manager Zsolt Ersek told Reuters the bank did not think the forint should strengthen further, as its current level was "sufficient for us to meet the several percent inflation target for the end of the year". \par \par \uc2  A NBH spokesman said further verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and an interest rate cut were possible if the forint firmed more. \par \par \uc2  But the impact of the NBH's words was short-lived. \par \par \uc2  On Monday the forint surged to a new all-time high at 11.50 percent above parity in its band. And on Tuesday it ended at 11.36 percent, firming over nine percent since its band was widened in early May. \par \par \uc2  "It was a pretty weak attempt at verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...if they really wanted to stop the forint from appreciating then they would have to be more persuasive," said Juliet Sampson at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  Sampson said the forint would firm further in the days ahead, but the NBH would wait with open market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tested the strong end of its trading band. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Mihaly Varga also said earlier on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not yet on the agenda. \par \par \uc2  However, most analysts say a "real" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come soon - possibly as early as this week. \par \par \uc2  "It could happen any time, as they (NBH) like to cause a surprise," said Istvan Zsoldos, analyst at Concorde Securities in Budapest. "It may happen as early as tomorrow, as a basic rule of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is that it should be unexpected." \par \par \uc2  Zsoldos said he expected the bank to step actively into the market when the forint rose to around 13 percent. \par \par \uc2  Gabor Bognar at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London also said the NBH will not allow the forint to test the top of its band, because then the bank's monetary policy would be just as limited as it was before the band widening. \par \par \uc2  "I expect that around 12-13 percent there will be an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which is more than a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Bognar said, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy was too open and the impacts of exchange rate changes too unpredictable for the forint to be allowed to rise much higher. \par \par \uc2  FORINT BEST CHOICE IN REGION \par \par \uc2  Hungary's robust economic growth and stable economy are two key factors attracting a massive flow of foreign capital to the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government securities markets. \par \par \uc2  Hungary is seen as the best choice in the region now - with Poland struggling with economic difficulties and political uncertainty due to an election and the Czech Republic trying to bring its budget deficit under control. \par \par \uc2  "If I were a portfolio investor, I would bring my short-term money to Hungary rather than Poland for now," Bognar said. \par \par \uc2  "The forint has outperformed in the last few weeks because it is catching up (with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region)," Sampson added. \par \par \uc2  Though analysts said it would be too early to estimate the size of a possible open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said even a low-scale move could restrain the forint's rise for a while. \par \par \uc2  "In such a speculative market with a small amount they can have a relatively significant impact," Zsoldos said. \par \par \uc2  "In this case (when the forint needs to be weakened) the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has practically unlimited munition to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. The only factor to consider would be an eventual rise in the money supply which may push up inflation. \par \par \uc2  The NBH said when it widened the forint's band that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation would help meet its target for seven percent year-on-year inflation by the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  The widening was part of a general overhaul in the monetary regime to prepare the forint for European Union (EU) and eurozone membership, expected by 2004 and 2006, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The overhaul included launching an inflation-targeting monetary policy and allowing foreigners to trade in short-term debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives. \par \par \uc2  Bognar at Dresdner said the NBH might cut its key two-week deposit rate when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  - initially by 25 basis points - depending on the actual size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I think if the size is more a few hundred millions of euros then a rate cut would also be considered by the bank," he said. \par \par \uc2  However, Zsoldos said the NBH would cut rates only around October, when inflation is seen falling to around 8 to 9 percent year on year - down from a peak at 10.8 percent in May. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6q00tmf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
