\par\par \b \uc2  Investors await Hungary decree on freeing forint. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sandor Peto \par \uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2001\par 07:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, June 15 (Reuters) - Hungarian markets were waiting on edge for a government decree on Friday, which is expected to make the forint convertible later in the day, fully opening its markets for the first time in 70 years, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The decree, widely expected for weeks, is unlikely to cause dramatic price rises after a recent sharp rise of the forint and bonds, but market participants said they would keep eyes open. \par \par \uc2  "Nobody knows yet what is the final text of the new law," a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said. "I think lawyers will read it as fast as they can and give advice to traders on what they can and cannot do... it will be a very interesting day." \par \par \uc2  The decree will lift short-term capital flow restrictions, allowing foreigners to trade derivatives and short-term debt. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said it could lure new investment fund players into Hungary, dominantly from Europe, but new U.S. investors - who like short-term investment opportunities - may also appear. \par \par \uc2  FOCUS ON FORINT AND BILLS \par \par \uc2  The market free-up will open business opportunities so far unseen in Hungary for investment funds, banks and companies. \par \par \uc2  In the past two weeks forint eurobond issues mushroomed, with foreign banks exploiting a strong interest in forint assets ahead of the free-up as the number of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Europe is decreasing because of the EU's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Interest is likely to focus on the forint and the short-term debt market, but in the future the half-dead derivatives market could also come to a buzzing life due to inflows, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "There are people who keep a substantial portfolio of bonds expiring in the coming months, and they may try to replace them with T-bills now," said Gabor Bekes, analyst at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  The forint rose 7.75 percent within its trading band, to end at 10 percent above parity on Friday, since it was widened on May 4 to 15 percent either side of parity, from 2.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the forint may firm further, but the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb it verbally or by commissioning a commercial bank to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The NBH cannot allow a forint rise to the top of the band as that could show that its exchange rate target may contradict its new inflation-targeting monetary policy, analysts added. \par \par \uc2  "The forint can firm further by one or two percent before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  begins to fear that large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be needed (at the top of the band)," said Bekes. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6f01l0v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
