\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary short bonds firm as foreigners buy. \b0\par\par\uc2 281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2001\par 02:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, May 8 (Reuters) - Hungarian short-term government bonds extended their gains on Tuesday as foreign investors continued to buy, encouraged by a firming of the forint in its trading band which was widened last week, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "I'm sure that five or 10 billion forints in net terms came in from abroad today and also yesterday," said Karoly Misky of Bondtrade, adding, however, it was difficult to predict the levels where foreign investors would take profits. \par \par \uc2  Short-term bond yields fell by some 15 basis points, with the 2001/C-series trading at around 10.60 percent 3:30 p.m./1330 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Longer bonds, however, were volatile, and retreated by about 10 basis points by the afternoon from Monday's levels as domestic investors were selling, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Local investors feared that a further firming of the forint could trigger selling by foreigners, traders said. Market rumours that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the forint firms to beyond a certain level also sparked selling, traders added. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted a stronger forint, but not to the extremes," one trader said. "An \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not an impossible idea." \par \par \uc2  National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) governor Zsigmond Jarai announced last Thursday a widening in the forint band to 15 percent either side of a central parity rate, set daily by the NBH, from 2.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Jarai said the bank would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market only if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reach each end of the new band. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday the forint closed at 5.75/5.80 percent above parity, compared with 4.9/5.07 percent above the midline on Monday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx5800jrp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
