\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary Jarai Vows No  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Intevention In New Wider Band \b0\par\par\uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2001\par 12:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST -(Dow Jones)- The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the forint's newly-widened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Zsigmond Jarai said Friday. \par \par \uc2  At a press conference, the governor also predicted the forint's monthly devaluation rate could be eliminated in the first half of 2002 if inflation falls. \par \par \uc2  In a surprise move late Thursday, the authorities widened the band to +/-15% around a midpoint fixed to the euro daily, from +/-2.25% previously. \par \par \uc2  Friday, the forint strengthened to about 4% on its strong side by midday. \par \par \uc2  "We didn't widen the band just to say we've widened it. In the first several weeks we'll definitely let the forint float within the band and we'll not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  even after that," Jarai said. \par \par \uc2  That contradicts dealers' expectations that the bank will maintain an 'inner band' and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep it there. \par \par \uc2  "Right now, we're seeing the forint strengthening about 2.5% (on its latest and firmest spot at 2.25% in the old band), and that's more or less in line with our expectations," Jarai said. "But it's too early to tell how things will develop. Friday's usually a casual Friday among dealers so we'll wait till next week to see what really happens." \par \par \uc2  While widening the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained its monthly devaluation of the forint's middle rate in the band by 0.2% against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Later, the bank wants to abolish the devaluation - or the 'crawling peg,' as it is known - probably next year, but only if inflation falls below 7%, said Jarai. \par \par \uc2  "We'll need to think about the crawl if inflation falls below 7%; there's no sense in devaluation below 7% inflation," he added. \par \par \uc2  "If annual inflation falls to 6-7% in December as we now predict, that signals a trend which allows us to eliminate the crawling peg, perhaps in the first half of next year," Jarai explained. \par \par \uc2  The authorities widened the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band to try to reduce persistently high inflation of around 10%, but it won't cool down this year, so they have maintained their 2001 inflation projection of 8-9%, and around 6-7% next year, Jarai said. \par \par \uc2  The wider band "won't affect Hungarian businesses' competitiveness this year," he said. "It won't change unemployment and it won't increase the current account deficit." \par \par \uc2  -By Balazs Koranyi, Dow Jones Newswires; +361-267-0623; balazs.koranyi@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx5401zyc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
