\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary Jarai:Wider Euro/Forint Band In 12-18 Mos-Paper \b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 11:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST -(Dow Jones)- The Hungarian forint's trading band could be widened in 12 to 18 months, recently appointed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Zsigmond Jarai told business daily Vilaggazdasag in an interview published Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "We could introduce an exchange rate system similar to ERM II earlier (than 2004), a year or a year and a half from now," Jarai was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  Under the European Monetary System's exchange rate mechanism, the ERM II, the forint would be fixed to the euro but allowed to fluctuate plus/minus 15% before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  hopes to join ERM II at the start of 2004, when it expects to join the European Union, Jarai added. \par \par \uc2  At present, the forint is traded in a narrow, plus or minus 2.25% band around its midpoint, which is fixed to the euro daily. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  devalues the midpoint 0.3% against the euro monthly. The bank will cut the devaluation rate, or crawling peg, to 0.2% from Apr. 1. \par \par \uc2  Jarai believes that the crawling peg system needs to remain in place until the need for devaluation is eliminated. \par \par \uc2  "I believe there's a need for two more cuts in the devaluation rate. At present, we don't want to widen the (trading) band because the (resulting) appreciation (of the forint) wouldn't be beneficial to the economy," Jarai said. \par \par \uc2  "Except for banks, the income increase wasn't so large (last year) in the corporate sector that it would stand a further appreciation of the forint," Jarai added. \par \par \uc2  The forint appreciated 5% against the euro last year in real terms. \par \par \uc2  "With growth slowing in the E.U., it wouldn't be right to take steps to strengthen the forint," Jarai added. \par \par \uc2  As Hungary's robust growth is driven by exports, a further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation would hinder its competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Jarai added that he sees real interest rates on the short end low at present, but not too low for the longer end of the bond curve. The governor noted though that "interest rates don't make such an impact on Hungary's economy as (in developed countries)." \par \par \uc2  The reason is that corporate loans only amount to 70% of gross domestic product while in western Europe the same indicator is 200%. Household loans are only 5% of consumption while the same indicator is around 100% in western Europe, Jarai said. \par \par \uc2  Regarding the current account, which posted a much higher-than-expected deficit in January, Jarai pointed out that this partly reflects high natural gas prices, which lag the rise of crude oil prices by about six months. \par \par \uc2  Newspaper website: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.vilaggazdasag.hu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.vilaggazdasag.hu}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Margit Feher, Dow Jones Newswires; +361-267-0622;\par margit.feher@dowjones.com\par -0- 08/03/01 10-54G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx38013jz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
