\par\par \b \uc2  MONDAY IN BRIEF - Hungary - Dec 22. \b0\par\par\uc2 543 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 1998\par 07:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2   --------- GENERAL NEWS ------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2  * Hungary's biggest railway union will hold a two-hour warning strike on Tuesday in support of their wage demand after engine drivers' strike on Monday, a union official said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  -FORINT- * \par \par \uc2  Hungary's forint is expected to continue to trade at the top end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket into next year, benefiting from the first sustained capital inflow since Russia's crisis, analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  * On Monday the forint closed at 1.75 percent above parity within the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (NBH) 4.5 percent wide \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band compared with .075 percent above parity at Friday's close. Against the dollar, the Hungarian unit closed at 213.50/70 compared with Friday's close of 215.00/10. \par \par \uc2  - INTEREST RATES - \par \par \uc2  * The National Bank of Hungary (NBH) said on Monday it cut all its three repo rates by 25 basis points each. The overnight repo rate was cut to 19.50 percent, the overnight reverse repo rate to 14.00 percent and one-month reverse repo rate to 16.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  * The National Bank of Hungary (NBH) surprised the markets on Monday by cutting all three repo rates by 25 basis points and analysts said more rate cuts would follow early next year if inflation continues to decline. \par \par \uc2  * Hungary's interbank overnight (O/N) rates ended slightly higher at 16.875 percent (before the NBH rate cuts) in very low trade on Monday after closing Friday at 16.75 percent, dealers said. \par \par \uc2   ----------- EQUITIES ----------------- \par \par \uc2  * Hungarian shares finished firmer on Monday in lively trade, driven by a rising trend in European markets and strong domestic macroeconomic fundamentals, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The BUX index finished at 6,111.20 points, up 213.38 points or 3.62 percent. Spot share turnover rose to 25.109 billion forints ($115.9 million) from 14.417 billion forints on Friday, the BSE said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  * Russia's Gazprom and Hungary's MOL signed a barter agreement under which Hungary will pay with food and drugs for up to one billion cubic meters of imported natural gas until the end of 1999, the clearing bank said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  * Hungarian regional electricity distributors Dedasz Rt and Titasz Rt have submitted their applications to the bourse to list their stocks on the Budapest Stock Exchange (BSE), the BSE said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  * Hungarian oil and gas group MOL said on Monday it signed a $150 million multi-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revolving syndicated loan agreement. \par \par \uc2  * Nomura Equities Research said on Monday it maintained a "buy" recommendation on shares on Hungarian brewery Brau Union Hungaria, saying it expected strong profit growth next year. \par \par \uc2   ----------- ECONOMIC INDICATORS ------ \par \par \uc2  * Hungary's gross domestic product rose 5.6 percent in the third quarter of the year from the same period of 1997 and was up by 5.1 percent year-on-year in the January-September period, the Central Statistical Office said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  * Hungary's third-quarter economic growth of 5.6 percent is higher than expected, but analysts said a strong rise in imports and a slowdown in export growth were warning signs for the current account. \par \par \uc2  ((Budapest newsroom ++ 36 1 327 4040 budapest.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926ducm01twv\par